The prelinked storage personality asks for its node by name

The photograph of run 585 says where the storage driver is.  The panel
reads

    nio: ufs probe #15 provider=cpu7 compatible="qcom,kryo"

and the NioUfs candidate carries the marks D1 and D2 but not D3 and D4.
D1 and D2 are what a probe leaves behind when it found nothing; D3 and D4
are what it leaves when it found something.  So the driver was offered a CPU
node, and said no.

That is not the driver's fault.  The personality that matched it says

    'IOClass'         = NioUfs;
    'IOProviderClass' = IOPlatformDevice;
    'IOProbeScore'    = 1000:32;

and nothing else.  With no name in it, the matching offers the driver every
IOPlatformDevice in the tree: the eight CPU nodes and every node under
/arm-io.  A CPU node says "qcom,kryo" and the UFS node says "qcom,ufshc",
and the driver knows only the second, so every one of them is a no.

This is the personality the matching uses.  It is in the prelinked
catalogue from the start -- libsa/bootstrap.cpp adds the built-in driver
personalities long before the matching sweep begins -- so the entry the
sweep finds when it offers the tree's nodes to the driver is this one, and
not the one the platform publishes later.

The name is the check the matching is built for.  IORegistryEntry::compareName()
compares the node's name for exact equality and does not look at
"compatible" at all, and the official documentation says the same: "The
adt-name values are the exact IONameMatch strings of the Apple drivers that
consume them.  IORegistryEntry::compareName() compares the node name for
exact equality and does not look at `compatible`".  This board's tree
builder writes the UFS host controller as the node "ufs"
(scripts/nio_adt.py), so that is the string the personality asks for.

Why it matters is the whole of the rest of the port.  The official boot
chain says the platform expert matches the ADT root node, the storage
driver publishes the userdata partition, the partition scheme publishes OS
and SystemOS, and the kernel matches the root volume by its UUID and mounts
it and starts launchd.  Every one of those steps after the first depends on
the storage driver being matched, and it never was: no userdata partition,
no IOMedia carrying the root volume, no root, no launchd.

The other personality for the same driver is left as it was: it matches the
platform expert, and the platform expert is the one that publishes the
tree's nodes in the first place.

